# mlo16277.dat

Dataset: dip-mlo — Mauna Loa Observatory: last direct sunlight of every evening of 2016 (NOAA GML)
  and the horizon towards the setting Sun (Copernicus DEM)
File: data/dip/mlo/raw/mlo16277.dat
SHA-256: ae9cc37b29fee3a72138f2b3e2d9232352cbcf9d997223c30a0130219f743d6f (also in
  data/dip/mlo/inputs.lock; check with: cd data/dip/mlo && sha256sum -c inputs.lock)
In the public repository: no, only this description and the hash: the file may be shared but is kept
  out of git for its size. Get it from fog fetch dip-mlo, then compare its SHA-256 with the one
  above.
Obtained: downloaded as published (fog fetch or by hand).
From: NOAA GML baseline radiation, Mauna Loa, one-minute means, 2016
  (gml.noaa.gov/aftp/data/radiation/baseline/mlo/).
Check it yourself: fog verify dip-mlo (tools/) downloads it again into a temporary directory and
  compares it with this hash.
Collected: 2026-10-03
Licence: other — NOAA GML baseline radiation data: public domain (US government). Copernicus DEM
  GLO-30: (c) DLR e.V. 2010-2014 and (c) Airbus Defence and Space GmbH 2014-2018, provided under
  COPERNICUS by the European Union and ESA; free licence.
Sources:
- NOAA Global Monitoring Laboratory, baseline surface radiation, Mauna Loa (MLO), one-minute means,
  2016 <https://gml.noaa.gov/aftp/data/radiation/baseline/mlo/>
- Copernicus DEM GLO-30 (ESA / Airbus), open bucket <https://copernicus-dem-30m.s3.amazonaws.com/>
Evidence class (rule 10): check
Where the Earth's radius enters:
- MLO_HORIZON, MLO_SEA, MLO_PROFILE: the horizon and the sea dip are computed on a sphere of radius
  6,371 km from the Copernicus DEM (heights over the WGS84/EGM2008 geoid)
What would vindicate the flat model (rule 5): Last light at the times a flat Sun at the model's
  height would give, or a sea horizon that never dips below the observatory's eye level.
Built by: fog build dip-mlo
